	abstract = {Background: Health practitioners across the globe are rapidly adopting social and mobile media. This adoption carries considerable risks and requires the development of new forms of digital professionalism. This study examined the use of a simple, smartphone-based tutorial which contained authentic text scenarios, reflective prompts, and questions aimed at the development of digital professionalism. The content of the tutorial drew on newly developed social and mobile media guidelines for Zambian nurses and midwives. Objectives: The first objective was to examine knowledge outcomes of the scenario-based mobile learning tutorial, its perceived usefulness, and ease of use and compare it with the study of guidelines on paper. The second objective was to determine whether socio-demographic factors, such as age, area, and gender would be linked to intervention outcomes Methods: A mixed methods, 2-arm, open label, parallel randomized controlled experiment was carried out. The experiment involved 58 Zambian nurses. Participants in the first arm (group) worked through the scenario-based mobile learning tutorial. Participants in the second group studied the paper guidelines. Post-intervention surveys contained a knowledge test to examine nurses' knowledge on digital professionalism, and further questions on usefulness, ease of use, and sociodemographic variables. Moreover, two focus groups (n=6/7) were conducted to explore the experience of nurses who used the scenario-based mobile learning tutorial. Results: Nurses' knowledge about digital professionalism and the perceived usefulness of the learning materials were significantly higher in the scenario-based mobile learning group - as compared to the paper guideline group. No differences were found regarding the ease of use. The perceived ease of use of the scenario-based mobile learning tutorial was positively associated with nurses' mobile phone competencies. And, in the paper guideline group, male user and users from urban areas found the materials less useful as compared to female users and user from non-urban areas. The qualitative analysis affirmed and further explained these findings, suggesting that the authenticity of the scenarios in combination with reflective prompts and questions triggered reflective practice. Conclusions: The findings provide tentative support for the use of a scenario-based mobile learning tutorial as a viable learning method to train some aspects of digital professionalism - in addition to the circulation of guidelines in the domain of public health. © 2021 JMIR Publications Inc.. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {digital literacy; digital professionalism; fake news; scenarios; social media professionalism},
	publisher = {JMIR Publications Inc.},
