	abstract = {This article investigates how a particular synthesis of hybridized conceptualizations and paradoxical ideologies emerged from the liminal conditions of Silicon Valley during a critical juncture of the so-called microprocessor revolution. The analysis predominantly features investigation of theoretical and cultural content from a historic nodal point occurring a quarter century ago. This mid-1990’s juncture appears in retrospect to represent a critical juncture where a unique fusion of technological utopianism, radical individualism and neoconservative entrepreneurial orthodoxy coalesce into a recognizable matrix of problematic – sometimes diametrically opposed – credos which have come to emblematize the culture of modern tech corporations and their various platforms in the contemporary information age. Subsequent discussion explores how the concept of cyberspace has systematically undermined concrete presence, replacing it with theatrical forms of self-presentation and performance; directly contributing to the post-truth, fake news or alternative facts era of cyberspace. Such developments are later characterized with respect to wider trends within art, entertainment and advertising occurring in parallel to the Silicon Valley phenomenon. © 2022 International Political Anthropology.},
