	abstract = {The emergence of automated journalism and concerns about the rise of ‘fake news’ are the latest catalysts for journalism educators to revisit how reporting and writing skills are taught. In this study, we investigate if and how journalism educators are revising their curriculum to address changes brought about by automation, with particular emphasis on its potential to help with news verification. Our results suggest that curricular changes are being driven by instructors teaching skills-based courses, who are also more likely to view automated journalism favorably compared to those teaching conceptual courses. Journalism educators in our study also expressed a need for more media literacy courses to better train students to understand the practice and significance of automated journalism. © 2020 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
