	affiliations = {Western Carolina University, 1 University Way, 305 Coulter Building, Cullowhee, 28723, NC, United States},
	abstract = {Though digital amplification is often understood to afford energy and reach, this article examines ways digital rhetors also use amplification to exhaust audiences. This article forwards rhetorical exhaustion as both effect and strategy, a frame for understanding how digital rhetors wear down rhetorical opponents. First, this article recovers the classical rhetorical figures of amplification and accumulation, highlighting their reliance on accumulation as a strategy and an ethics of concern for audiences’ energies. Next, this article analyzes the 2016 election interference strategies of the Russian Internet Research Agency (IRA). Analysis of two US Senate Intelligence Committee commissioned reports shows how the IRA worked to energize audiences through outrage, then sought to exhaust Black audiences’ energies for civic engagement. The IRA practiced slow circulation through rhetorical persistence to create a civic affect of cynicism aimed at demotivating audiences. Rhetorical exhaustion highlights the productive potential of exhaustion—as rhetorical effect, as strategic uses of digital amplification, and as an ethical concern for digital rhetors. As such, rhetorical exhaustion offers a useful counter to paradigms of viral circulation and fake news in 21<sup>st</sup> century digital circulation. © 2020 Elsevier Inc.},
