	abstract = {Democratic nations prosper when the legislature, executive and judiciary and media function with utmost responsibility. India is the largest democracy and media played the role of vanguard during its freedom struggle and later as a watchdog and whistleblower, with keen grasp of nation-building activity, strengthening democratic institutions. Media’s primary task is to provide unbiased information, critical insight, space for democratic debate and importantly voice to voiceless and keep vigil on government’s omissions and commissions. Though media is independent, cross-media ownership and politicians or corporate owning media houses is defeating this feature, stifling voice of marginalized sections, ignoring their voices. Corporate media launch misinformation campaign, especially during elections, hijacking electoral politics, destroying the democratic fabric of the society. People were denied access to information. Paid news has emerged in recent elections in Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh, exposing the nexus between political parties and ‘independent’ media, undermining the integrity, honesty and independence of media. No effective remedial mechanisms were evolved to curb this menace. A diversified and free media with greater social responsibility and accountability is crucial for strengthening democracy in India. © Symbiosis Pune.},
